---
---

<section id="faq" class="bg-canvas py-20 w-full" style="scroll-margin-top: 80px;">
	<div class="w-full px-lg" style="margin: 0 auto; max-width: 48rem;">
		<h2 class="text-display-lg mb-12">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

		<div style="border-top: 1px solid rgb(235, 235, 235);">
			<!-- FAQ Item 1 -->
			<details class="group cursor-pointer" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(235, 235, 235);">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-4 hover:opacity-80 transition-colors">
					What is a contrast ratio?
					<svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" style="transition: transform 0.2s;"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body pb-4" style="color: rgb(77, 77, 77);">
					Contrast ratio is the difference in luminance (brightness) between two colors. It's measured on a scale from 1:1 (no contrast) to 21:1 (maximum contrast). Higher ratios mean better readability.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 2 -->
			<details class="group cursor-pointer" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(235, 235, 235);">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-4 hover:opacity-80 transition-colors">
					Why does contrast matter for accessibility?
					<svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" style="transition: transform 0.2s;"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body pb-4" style="color: rgb(77, 77, 77);">
					Low contrast makes text harder or impossible to read for people with low vision, color blindness, and other visual impairments. It also affects all users in bright sunlight or on older displays. Sufficient contrast is essential for legal accessibility compliance (WCAG).
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 3 -->
			<details class="group cursor-pointer" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(235, 235, 235);">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-4 hover:opacity-80 transition-colors">
					What's the difference between AA and AAA?
					<svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" style="transition: transform 0.2s;"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body pb-4" style="color: rgb(77, 77, 77);">
					<strong>AA</strong> is the baseline standard required by law in most countries (ADA, EN 301 549, etc.). <strong>AAA</strong> is enhanced and recommended for sensitive content like medical, educational, or critical information. Most websites target AA.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 4 -->
			<details class="group cursor-pointer" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(235, 235, 235);">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-4 hover:opacity-80 transition-colors">
					What counts as "large text"?
					<svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" style="transition: transform 0.2s;"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body pb-4" style="color: rgb(77, 77, 77);">
					Per WCAG 2.2, large text is: 18px (or larger) AND 400 weight, OR 14px (or larger) AND 700 weight. Large text requires a lower contrast ratio (3:1 AA, 4.5:1 AAA) because size aids readability.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 5 -->
			<details class="group cursor-pointer" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(235, 235, 235);">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-4 hover:opacity-80 transition-colors">
					Does color blindness affect contrast requirements?
					<svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" style="transition: transform 0.2s;"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body pb-4" style="color: rgb(77, 77, 77);">
					Contrast ratio is based on luminance, not color. So a red/green pair with sufficient luminance contrast will meet WCAG even if a colorblind user can't distinguish the colors. However, you should never use color alone to convey information. Always add labels, patterns, or icons.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 6 -->
			<details class="group cursor-pointer" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(235, 235, 235);">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-4 hover:opacity-80 transition-colors">
					How do I test my website's contrast?
					<svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" style="transition: transform 0.2s;"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body pb-4" style="color: rgb(77, 77, 77);">
					Use ContrastLab to test individual color pairs. For whole pages, use browser extensions like WAVE, Axe DevTools, or Lighthouse (built into Chrome DevTools). Test in real-world conditions: different lighting, older devices, and different browsers.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 7 -->
			<details class="group cursor-pointer" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(235, 235, 235);">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-4 hover:opacity-80 transition-colors">
					Can I export my color checks?
					<svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" style="transition: transform 0.2s;"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body pb-4" style="color: rgb(77, 77, 77);">
					Yes! ContrastLab generates shareable URLs for any color pair. You can also export CSS variables and Tailwind classes directly. Save your favorite palettes for reuse. API and bulk export (CSV, JSON) are coming soon.
				</p>
			</details>

			<!-- FAQ Item 8 -->
			<details class="group cursor-pointer" style="border-bottom: 1px solid rgb(235, 235, 235);">
				<summary class="text-body-md-strong flex items-center justify-between py-4 hover:opacity-80 transition-colors">
					What about WCAG 3.0 and APCA?
					<svg width="20" height="20" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" style="transition: transform 0.2s;"><polyline points="6 9 12 15 18 9"></polyline></svg>
				</summary>
				<p class="text-body-md text-body pb-4" style="color: rgb(77, 77, 77);">
					WCAG 3.0 is in development and will use APCA (Advanced Perceptual Contrast Algorithm), which is more accurate for modern displays. ContrastLab is built with future standards in mind. WCAG 2.2 remains the current legal standard.
				</p>
			</details>
		</div>
	</div>
</section>

<style>
	details summary {
		list-style: none;
	}

	details summary::-webkit-details-marker {
		display: none;
	}

	details[open] summary svg {
		transform: rotate(180deg);
	}
</style>
